            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"In biblical symbolism, a 'name' represents the essential character, identity, authority, presence, and reputation of the person who bears it.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"A 'name' symbolizes the covenant identity of a house, which specifically consists of a triad: the Head's Word, covenant law, and a fruitful priesthood.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Personal character, identity, and authority.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A tripartite covenant identity (Word, law, priesthood) belonging to a house.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book rejects the generalized definition of name as character or reputation, restricting it exclusively to a structural covenantal triad.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus defines the Name as standing for the person himself and his attributes. The book defines the Name as a composite of three specific covenantal elements based on the contents of the Ark of the Covenant.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book completely redefines the symbol, moving away from the consensus view of personal identity/authority to a highly specific, typological construct derived from the contents of the Ark (manna, tables of stone, Aaron's rod) housed in the temple built 'for the Name.'\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The fact that the temple was built 'for the Name' and contained the Ark does not mean the word 'Name' itself symbolizes the three items inside the Ark. This interpretation confuses an object associated with God's presence for the definition of the word 'name,' ignoring hundreds of contexts where 'name' clearly means personal identity, reputation, or authority.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
